{
  "id": "COMPOUND:14824 - PROTEIN:6324",
  "verbalization": "Heparin is implicated in the regulation of M-phase phosphoprotein 8 through a series of target-mediated pathways. The evidence suggests that heparin acts as a target of multiple compounds, which in turn target M-phase phosphoprotein 8. Specifically, heparin is linked to SNU-387, a cell line that is a target of several compounds, including Quercetagetin 3-methyl ether 7-glucoside, 2-(hydroxymethyl)-3-[(E)-prop-1-enyl]phenol, and 5-(2-Hydroxyacetyl)-10H-Phenazine-1-Carboxylic Acid. These compounds all share a common mechanism of action, targeting M-phase phosphoprotein 8, which is a key regulator of the cell cycle. The integration of these pathways indicates that heparin may influence the activity of M-phase phosphoprotein 8 through the action of these compounds, potentially affecting cell cycle progression. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between heparin and M-phase phosphoprotein 8, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
